<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
	<!--
	  macOS only — iOS has no sandbox entitlements file, and none of this
	  applies there.

	  network.client is not optional: this app exists to talk to the
	  family's own server, and a sandboxed Mac app without it cannot open
	  a socket at all. (The same entitlement, missed, is what crashed
	  md.macOS's WebContent process on launch.)

	  user-selected.read-WRITE covers both directions of "a file the person
	  chose in a panel, and nothing else": the NSOpenPanel that attaches a
	  file, and the NSSavePanel behind the viewer's "Save a copy". It was
	  read-only until 2026-08-31, which made that Save a copy a dead
	  control — the copy was refused and both `try?`s threw the refusal
	  away, so the button did nothing and said nothing. read-write subsumes
	  read, so the open panel is unaffected.

	  There is still deliberately no downloads-folder or documents-folder
	  entitlement: this widens what the person can pick, not what the app
	  can reach on its own. Everything the app writes unasked goes to its
	  own container, and anything leaving it goes through the share sheet,
	  which needs no entitlement at all.
	-->
	<!--
	  Push. Same note as the iOS entitlements: "development" is what
	  source carries, and Xcode rewrites it to "production" when an
	  archive is exported.

	  THE KEY NAME IS NOT THE iOS ONE. macOS spells this
	  `com.apple.developer.aps-environment`; iOS spells it `aps-environment`.
	  Both Mac provisioning profiles grant the macOS spelling only — the
	  development profile with "development", the store profile with
	  "production" — so a request for the iOS key matches nothing and Xcode
	  drops it while signing, WITHOUT failing the build. That is how build
	  100 reached TestFlight with no push entitlement of any spelling in the
	  binary: a quit Mac was never woken, while in-app banners kept working
	  because ChatNotifier raises those locally off the live socket, so
	  nothing looked broken from inside the app.

	  Check the product, not this file, after touching it:
	      codesign -d --entitlements - --xml <FamilyConnect.app> | plutil -p -
	-->
	<key>com.apple.developer.aps-environment</key>
	<string>development</string>
	<key>com.apple.security.app-sandbox</key>
	<true/>
	<key>com.apple.security.network.client</key>
	<true/>
	<!-- Voice calls are peer to peer: WebRTC binds a UDP socket and the
	     other side's ICE checks arrive on it, which the sandbox counts as
	     serving. Without this a call gathers host candidates and hears
	     nothing on them. -->
	<key>com.apple.security.network.server</key>
	<true/>
	<key>com.apple.security.files.user-selected.read-write</key>
	<true/>
	<!--
	  Recording a voice note. A sandboxed Mac app WITHOUT this does not
	  fail: AVCaptureDevice reports the microphone as authorised and the
	  recorder writes a file of silence, which is a far worse failure than
	  a refusal.
	-->
	<key>com.apple.security.device.audio-input</key>
	<true/>
	<!-- Video calls (docs/protocol.md, "Video"). Same failure shape as the
	     microphone: a sandboxed Mac app WITHOUT this is not refused — the
	     capture session "runs" and the far side sees black, which is worse
	     than a denial. The permission prompt itself still gates use. -->
	<key>com.apple.security.device.camera</key>
	<true/>
	<!-- Sharing a location. Without this a sandboxed Mac app is refused by
	     CoreLocation, and the refusal arrives as a plain denial with
	     nothing to say why — the same shape as the missing audio-input
	     entitlement, which reported success and then recorded silence. -->
	<key>com.apple.security.personal-information.location</key>
	<true/>
	<!-- The share extension stages files into this group's ShareInbox/
	     for the app to pick up (ShareImport). -->
	<key>com.apple.security.application-groups</key>
	<array>
		<string>group.me.nettrash.FamilyConnect</string>
	</array>
</dict>
</plist>
